# Instrumental variables and the LATE theorem
next: {"title":"Bad instruments, weak instruments","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/bad-instruments-weak-instruments.cast"}

## The Lottery as an Instrument
voice: female
level: advanced
use: causal_dag
with:
    nodes:
      - {name: Lottery Z, role: exposure}
      - {name: Coverage D, role: mediator}
      - {name: Outcome Y, role: outcome}
      - {name: Unobserved U, role: confounder}
    edges: Lottery Z -> Coverage D; Coverage D -> Outcome Y; Unobserved U -> Coverage D; Unobserved U -> Outcome Y
    highlight_backdoor: true
    title: 'Oregon 2008: a lottery as an instrument'

Across these five parts we turn one lottery into an estimate of what health insurance does — and find out whose effect we actually end up holding.
    draw title node_lottery_z

Oregon, 2008: money to cover about ten thousand adults, ninety thousand applicants, and about thirty thousand names drawn at random — the only randomized trial of Medicaid coverage the United States has ever run.
    text n_applied "≈ 90,000 applied" x 200 y 175 font_size 24
    text n_drawn "≈ 30,000 names drawn" x 200 y 135 font_size 24

    pause 0.3

Winning the draw, Z_i, only bought the right to apply — it moves coverage D_i, it does not decide it.
    draw node_coverage_d edge_0

Only about one in four of the people whose names came up ended up enrolled.
    text n_enrolled "only ≈ 1 in 4 winners enrolled" #b5482e x 210 y 95 font_size 24

So the first stage — the jump in coverage caused by winning — is roughly a quarter, not one.
    text fs "First stage ≈ 0.25" #2f6b8f x 740 y 175 font_size 24

    pause 0.4

Coverage then acts on Y_i: emergency-department use, out-of-pocket spending, blood pressure, depression.
    draw node_outcome_y edge_1

    wait

But D_i itself was never randomized — the people who finished the paperwork differ in health, in organisation, in everything we never observe.
    draw node_unobserved_u edge_2 edge_3

These two arrows are the back door: regress Y on D and you blend the effect of coverage with the character of whoever took it up.
    focus node_unobserved_u edge_2 edge_3

    ask "Click the node whose arrows make a naive regression of Y on D biased." widget click answer node_unobserved_u
        intro: A quick check on the diagram.
        right: The unobserved traits U feed both coverage and outcomes — exactly the selection bias we decomposed in lecture two.
        wrong: Look for the variable with an arrow into D and an arrow into Y.

The lottery number is different in kind: a random-number machine assigned it, so nothing flows into Z.
    highlight node_lottery_z effect glow

    wait

    erase n_applied n_drawn

Comparing everyone drawn with everyone not drawn gives the intention-to-treat — the causal effect of winning a lottery.
    text itt "ITT = E[Y | Z=1] − E[Y | Z=0]" x 740 y 135 font_size 22

Honest, but it answers a question nobody asked: it averages the enrolled quarter together with three quarters whose coverage never budged.
    highlight itt effect pulse

    pause 0.3

If the lottery only moved a quarter of people, then scale the ITT by that quarter — divide by 0.25.
    text ask "ITT ÷ 0.25 = effect of coverage — for whom?" x 500 y 45 font_size 26
    mark mark target ask kind box
    delivery grave

    quiz "Winning cut out-of-pocket spending by $100 per person drawn, and raised coverage by 25 points. Rescaled, what is the effect per person whose coverage changed?" intro "Try the arithmetic."
        right: Dividing the $100 ITT by the 0.25 first stage gives $400 — the effect per person the lottery actually moved into coverage.
        wrong: Divide the reduced form by the first stage, don't multiply.
        * $25
        * $100
        + $400

That ratio is the Wald estimator — and the diagram already tells you its price: the number belongs to the quarter the lottery could move, not to the ninety thousand who applied.

## Four Assumptions on One Diagram
voice: female
level: advanced
use: causal_dag
with:
    nodes:
      - {name: Lottery Z, role: exposure}
      - {name: Coverage D, role: mediator}
      - {name: Outcome Y, role: outcome}
      - {name: Health U, role: confounder}
    edges: Lottery Z -> Coverage D; Coverage D -> Outcome Y; Health U -> Coverage D; Health U -> Outcome Y
    highlight_backdoor: true
    title: What the lottery must satisfy

Now that we have seen Oregon's lottery act as an instrument, the question is what could break it — because three of the four things it needs are not testable.
    draw title node_lottery_z node_label_lottery_z node_coverage_d node_label_coverage_d node_outcome_y node_label_outcome_y parallel true

The drawn name Z moves Medicaid coverage D, and coverage moves the outcome Y — that chain is the entire design.
    draw edge_0 edge_1

    pause 0.3

And here is why we needed an instrument at all: unobserved health drives both who enrolls and what they spend.
    draw node_health_u node_label_health_u edge_2 edge_3

    ask "Click the arrow that must be non-zero for the design to work at all." answer edge_0 widget click
        intro: Before we name the assumptions, one check.
        right: The lottery-to-coverage arrow — that is the first stage, and everything else divides by it.
        wrong: It is the first link, from the lottery to coverage; without it there is nothing to scale by.

    wait

Relevance first: the instrument has to actually move treatment, and in Oregon it did — of the thirty thousand names drawn, roughly a quarter ended up enrolled, a first stage of about twenty-five percentage points.
    label lab_relevance "Relevance: Cov(Z, D) ≠ 0" above #2f6b8f attach_to edge_0

What it rules out is a dead first stage: if the letters had shifted nobody's coverage, this arrow is zero and the design has nothing to divide by.
    highlight edge_0 effect glow

    pause 0.3

Independence: the draw is as good as random, so who wins is statistically unrelated to potential outcomes and potential treatment status.
    label lab_independence "Independence: Z ⊥ potential outcomes" below #2f6b8f attach_to node_lottery_z

It rules out a rigged draw — the sickest applicants quietly moved up the list would pour unobserved health straight back into Z.
    focus node_lottery_z edge_2 edge_3

    pause 0.3

Exclusion is the claim that this red arrow does not exist: the letter may change coverage, but it must not reach the outcome any other way.
    arrow excl_arrow node_lottery_z -> node_outcome_y curved dashed #b5482e

In Oregon that rules out the winning letter working on its own — a nudge that sends someone to a clinic, or relief from financial worry, in a person who never actually enrolled.
    mark excl_cross #b5482e target excl_arrow kind cross
    label lab_exclusion "Exclusion: no direct path Z → Y" below #2f6b8f attach_to excl_arrow

    quiz "Winning letters make people call a clinic, including those who never enroll. Which assumption fails?"
        intro: A quick test of that one.
        right: Exclusion fails — the instrument reaches Y through a channel that is not coverage.
        wrong: The lottery still moves coverage and is still random; the trouble is a second path to the outcome.
        * Relevance
        * Independence
        + Exclusion
        * Monotonicity

    wait

Monotonicity: the instrument pushes everyone in the same direction, so winning can only weakly increase your chance of being covered.
    label lab_monotonicity "Monotonicity: D_i(1) ≥ D_i(0) for all i" below #2f6b8f attach_to node_coverage_d

It rules out defiers — people who would enroll only if they lost; their responses enter the ratio with the opposite sign and can cancel compliers out entirely.
    highlight node_coverage_d effect glow delivery grave

    quiz "Someone who takes up Medicaid only when they lose the lottery is called a…"
        right: A defier — monotonicity assumes such people do not exist, because their negative responses net out against the compliers.
        wrong: Compliers follow the instrument and always-takers ignore it; this person moves against it.
        * Complier
        * Always-taker
        + Defier

These four conditions were assembled into a theorem by Guido Imbens and Joshua Angrist in 1994 — work that took both of them to Stockholm in 2021.
    portrait imbens of "Guido Imbens" cameo true

Relevance and independence make the ratio well-defined and unconfounded; exclusion and monotonicity are what turn it into a causal effect.
    erase imbens

Only the first is visible in the data — the other three are assumptions you defend, and they buy you an effect for the compliers alone.
    point at.ref node_coverage_d gesture circle

## Compliers: Countable, Never Nameable
voice: female
level: advanced
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: 'Enrolls if drawn:  D_i(1)'
    col_label: 'Enrolls if not drawn:  D_i(0)'
    row_values:
      - D_i(1) = 1
      - D_i(1) = 0
    col_values:
      - D_i(0) = 1
      - D_i(0) = 0
    cells:
      - [Always-taker, Complier]
      - [Defier, Never-taker]
    cell_notes:
      - [≈ 1 in 100, ≈ 24 in 100]
      - [ruled out, ≈ 75 in 100]
    highlight:
      - [0, 1]
    title: Four compliance types

    mark defier_x target cell_1_0 kind cross hidden true
    text first_stage_eq "P(D=1 | Z=1) − P(D=1 | Z=0)  =  0.25 − 0.01  =  0.24" #b5482e x 500 y 92 font_size 24 hidden true
    text head_obs "What the data shows you" x 280 y 650 font_size 28 hidden true
    text head_type "Which type is it?" x 740 y 650 font_size 28 hidden true
    text r1a "Z=1, D=1   drawn, enrolled" x 280 y 560 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r1b "Complier or Always-taker" #b5482e x 740 y 560 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r2a "Z=1, D=0   drawn, stayed out" x 280 y 480 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r2b "Never-taker" #2f6b8f x 740 y 480 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r3a "Z=0, D=1   not drawn, enrolled" x 280 y 400 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r3b "Always-taker" #2f6b8f x 740 y 400 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r4a "Z=0, D=0   not drawn, stayed out" x 280 y 320 font_size 23 hidden true
    text r4b "Complier or Never-taker" #b5482e x 740 y 320 font_size 23 hidden true

Now that we have the four assumptions on one diagram, the awkward question: the Oregon estimate belongs to a subgroup we can size to the percentage point and never identify by name.
    draw title grid row_title col_title

Every applicant carries two potential treatment states: D_i(1), would she enroll if her name were drawn, and D_i(0), would she enroll if it were not.
    draw row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1 parallel true

    pause 0.3

The diagonal holds the people the letter cannot move: always-takers, who find their way onto Medicaid regardless, and never-takers, who stay out either way — paperwork, stigma, or simply feeling healthy.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_1_1

Off the diagonal sit the compliers: D_i(1) equals one, D_i(0) equals zero — enrolled only because the envelope arrived.
    draw cell_0_1 hl_0_1

    pause 0.4

And here would be the defiers — perverse types who would enroll only if the lottery passed them over.
    draw cell_1_0

Monotonicity is exactly the assumption that empties this cell: winning can push you toward coverage, never away from it.
    draw defier_x delivery grave

    wait

Read the top row: everyone in it enrolls when drawn, so the 25 percent enrollment rate among winners counts compliers and always-takers together.
    focus cell_0_0 cell_0_1

Read the left column: these enroll with no letter at all — the roughly 1 percent among the not-drawn, and with defiers gone, that column is pure always-takers.
    focus cell_0_0 cell_1_0

Subtract one from the other and the always-takers cancel: the first stage, about 0.24, is nothing but the complier share.
    draw first_stage_eq

    quiz "Oregon's first stage is about 0.24. What is that number measuring?" intro "A quick check." required true
        right: E[D|Z=1] − E[D|Z=0] differences out the always-takers, so under monotonicity it is exactly the complier share.
        wrong: It is a difference in enrollment rates, not a level and not an outcome effect.
        * The effect of coverage on health
        + The share of applicants who are compliers
        * The share of applicants who enrolled

    wait

This partition into four types is Imbens and Angrist's 1994 move — the step that turned instrumental variables from an algebraic trick into a statement about people.
    clear

So take the four things the data can actually show you about one applicant.
    draw head_obs head_type parallel true

Two of them pin a type down exactly: a winner who stayed out must be a never-taker, a non-winner who enrolled must be an always-taker.
    draw r2a r2b r3a r3b parallel true

    pause 0.3

The other two are mixtures — and both of them are where the compliers hide.
    draw r1a r1b r4a r4b parallel true

Compliance type is itself a pair of potential outcomes, so the fundamental problem of causal inference bites at the first stage too: we see D_i only at the Z_i that person happened to draw.
    highlight r1b r4b effect glow

    wait

So no subsample you can construct is the complier subsample — and any regression run on "the enrollees" is running on a mixture.
    text synth "No observed cell is ever pure compliers." #b5482e x 500 y 190 font_size 28
    mark synth_box target synth kind box
    delivery grave

    quiz "Why can't we identify individual compliers?" required true
        right: Type is defined by both potential treatments, and each person reveals only one of them.
        wrong: Randomization holds fine, and 24 in 100 is plenty — the obstacle is that each person reveals only one potential treatment.
        * The lottery wasn't truly random
        + We would need both D_i(1) and D_i(0) for the same person
        * Compliers are too few to detect

Compliers are a population we can weigh and describe on average, but never enumerate — which is why the next step recovers their effect by arithmetic rather than by sorting people.

## What LATE buys you, and what it costs
voice: female
level: advanced
use: icon_array
with:
    total: 100
    groups:
      - {label: Compliers, count: 25, color: supply}
    rest_label: Always- & never-takers
    title: Whom does LATE speak for?

Now that we have seen the Wald ratio return a complier average causal effect, the question that decides how you may cite it: whom do these hundred Oregon applicants let you speak about?
    draw title rest legend_rest

    pause 0.3

Relevance first: the lottery has to move coverage, and it does — being drawn raised enrolment by about twenty-five percentage points, these twenty-five in a hundred.
    draw group_0 legend_0

Independence: the draw was random, so drawn and not-drawn hold the same mixture of types — it rules out Oregon quietly steering slots toward the sickest applicants.
    highlight group_0 rest effect glow

Exclusion: the letter may touch health only through coverage — for these seventy-five, whose D_i the letter never moved, Y_i must be untouched; no relief at winning, no outreach riding along with the envelope.
    highlight rest effect glow

Monotonicity is why there is no fourth colour on this page: no defiers, nobody who would have enrolled only if they had lost.
    point at.ref legend_rest gesture underline

    quiz "Which assumption rules out the letter improving health through anything other than coverage?"
        intro: A quick check on the four.
        right: Exclusion: the instrument may enter the outcome only through D_i.
        wrong: Independence buys you comparable groups; it is exclusion that forbids any other channel.
        * Relevance
        * Independence
        + Exclusion
        * Monotonicity

    wait

The compliers are the twenty-five with D_i(1) = 1 and D_i(0) = 0 — enrolled because the letter came, and only because it came.
    focus group_0 legend_0

We can count them exactly: enrolment among the drawn minus enrolment among the not-drawn — the always-takers, who enrol either way, cancel out of that difference.
    highlight legend_0 effect glow

Yet point at any one enrolled applicant and you cannot tell a complier from an always-taker: we see one of D_i(1), D_i(0), never both, so the colour here is bookkeeping, not a badge anyone in Oregon wears.
    point at.ref group_0 gesture circle

    ask "Click on the group whose average effect the Wald ratio identifies." intro "Your turn." answer group_0 widget click
        right: The compliers — the only people whose treatment status the instrument actually moved.

    quiz "Why can we never name an individual complier?"
        right: Compliance is defined by a pair, D_i(1) and D_i(0), and only one of the two is ever observed.
        wrong: It is not a data problem: complier status is defined by two potential treatments, and only one is ever realized.
        * Their survey data were lost
        + We only ever see one potential treatment status
        * The lottery records are sealed

    wait

Now the division. The reduced form is the letter's effect on Y_i across all hundred — diluted, because for these seventy-five the letter changed no coverage at all.
    highlight rest effect glow

By exclusion they add zero to the numerator and by definition zero to the denominator, so dividing by the first stage of 0.25 rescales a whole-sample effect onto the only people who could have produced it.
    highlight group_0 effect glow

    quiz "Holding the reduced form fixed, doubling the first stage to 0.5 would make LATE…"
        right: Half as large: the same intention-to-treat effect is now spread over twice as many compliers.
        wrong: The ratio divides by the first stage, so a larger denominator shrinks the estimate — the same ITT carried by twice as many people.
        * Twice as large
        + Half as large
        * Unchanged

That is Imbens and Angrist's local average treatment effect: not the effect on the insured, not on Oregon's uninsured, on these twenty-five.
    mark mark_late target legend_0 kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 0.5

And the locality is the price — change the instrument and you change the subgroup: a mandate speaks for people near the always-takers, an outreach campaign for the most reluctant, and both may report different numbers with neither being wrong.

It is also how to read Oregon itself — sharply lower depression and catastrophic expenses, a precise near-zero for blood sugar — as statements about lottery compliers, not about coverage in general.
    highlight group_0 legend_0 effect pulse

Across these five parts: four assumptions turn a lottery into an instrument, they carve out a subgroup we can count but never name, and the Wald ratio hands us their average effect — credibility bought by knowing exactly whom you have measured.

